## Why This Is Asked
Tests tenant isolation, cross‑account access, and rapid containment in a simple, reproducible pattern.

## Key Concepts
- Cross‑account IAM roles with restricted trust
- S3 per‑tenant prefixes and least privilege in bucket policies
- Server‑side encryption (SSE‑KMS) and key rotation
- Immutable auditability and rapid containment of breaches

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example trust policy snippet (Account B role trusting Account A data role)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": "sts:AssumeRole",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_A:role/DataReaderForTenant*",
      "Condition": {"StringEquals": {"sts:ExternalId": "tenant-<id>-read"}}
    }
  ]
}
```

```javascript
// Example bucket policy restricting to tenant prefix
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_B:role/AnalyticsRead"},
      "Action": "s3:GetObject",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::tenant-data-bucket/tenant-<id>/*"
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you rotate keys per tenant and handle revocation?
- How would you automate breach simulations and containment checks?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests fundamental cross-account trust, basic least privilege, and incident containment at beginner level.

## Key Concepts
- Cross-account IAM role and trust relationships
- S3 bucket policy and KMS encryption, per-tenant CMK
- Credential rotation and rapid containment

## Code Example
```json
// Trust policy snippet (tenant account -> analytics role)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::ANALYTICS_ACCOUNT_ID:role/TenantAnalyticsRole"},
      "Action": "sts:AssumeRole",
      "Condition": {"StringEquals": {"sts:ExternalId": "tenant-123-ext"}}
    }
  ]
}
```

```json
// Per-tenant bucket access policy (simplified)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["s3:GetObject"],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::tenant-123-data/*",
      "Condition": {"StringEquals": {"aws:SourceArn": "arn:aws:iam::ANALYTICS_ACCOUNT_ID:user/tenant-123"}}
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this to handle 2+ tenants with minimal policy duplication?
- What monitoring would you add to detect misconfigurations or breaches?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests cross‑account trust, least privilege, and basic auditability in a concrete, beginner‑friendly scenario.

## Key Concepts
- Cross‑account IAM roles
- Trust policy and restrictive permissions
- DynamoDB granular access
- STS session duration and credential rotation
- Auditing with CloudTrail

## Code Example
```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "RoleName": "LambdaToAccountBLogsRole",
  "AssumeRolePolicyDocument": {
    "Version": "2012-10-17",
    "Statement": [
      {
        "Effect": "Allow",
        "Principal": {"Service": "lambda.amazonaws.com"},
        "Action": "sts:AssumeRole"
      }
    ]
  },
  "Policies": [
    {
      "PolicyName": "DynamoDBPutItem",
      "PolicyDocument": {
        "Version": "2012-10-17",
        "Statement": [
          {
            "Effect": "Allow",
            "Action": ["dynamodb:PutItem"],
            "Resource": "arn:aws:dynamodb:region:acctB:table/LogTable",
